  "worldLore": {
    "Map Scale and Travel": {
      "text": "The Open Meridian realm is an intergalactic overview. Its regions are galaxies and its location pins are useful system-scale anchors, not every star. Major authored planets can open a separate surface realm; smaller worlds remain complex locations with landing, settlement, wilderness, and orbital areas. Passing ships, temporary camps, anomalies, wrecks, and minor stations stay narrative encounters until play makes them persistent.\n\nOrdinary travel time depends on distance, ship, corridor, traffic, and weather. Local docking can take minutes to hours; travel inside a system hours to days; a charted fold passage hours to several days; sparse multi-leg travel days to weeks. Compress routine travel after costs and meaningful choices are settled. Do not add an encounter merely because time passed."
    },
    "Technology Baseline": {
      "text": "Technology is material and fallible: fusion power, fold drives, artificial gravity in larger vessels, pressure habitats, cybernetics, gene medicine, drones, expert systems, sentient Synthborn, energy and kinetic weapons, strong sensors, and industrial fabrication. There is no magic, Force, psychic power, prophecy, teleportation, resurrection, perfect invisibility, instant universal communication, infinite energy, or machine that knows motives. Advanced technology needs power, access, cooling, maintenance, inputs, and time."
    },
    "Fold Corridors and Communication": {
      "text": "Fold drives use charted corridors shaped by mass and changing field conditions. A ship needs a working drive, a valid solution, fuel reserve, and a safe entry window. Gates are traffic and navigation anchors rather than portals that carry ships for free. Relay communication can be near-real-time in dense core routes and delayed by hours, days, or courier carriage on the frontier. No character receives remote knowledge before a message can plausibly arrive."
    },
    "Economy and Price Bands": {
      "text": "Credits are account-backed currency. Every good and service has a price even when the exact number is generated locally. Baselines: street meal 8-25; room 35-300 per night; ordinary clothing 60-300; monthly modest housing 700-2,500; basic clinic visit 120-600; serious treatment 2,000-40,000; local transit 3-80; interplanetary passenger berth 300-3,000; interstellar berth 1,500-15,000; docking 50-5,000 by hull; fuel 100-50,000 by hull and route; small repair 200-5,000; major ship repair thousands to millions.\n\nUse an authored item price as the normal-market baseline. Local abundance multiplies 0.7-0.9, normal supply 1.0, scarcity 1.2-1.8, crisis 2-4, and illegal access 1.5-5 plus risk. Taxes, licensing, faction relations, condition, urgency, and bargaining can change the final total. State the price before payment."
    },
    "Work and Contract Pay": {
      "text": "Routine unskilled shift: 80-180 credits. Skilled shift: 250-700. Licensed specialist day: 600-2,000. Local courier or repair job: 300-4,000. Interplanetary cargo: 2,000-25,000 before operating costs. Interstellar cargo: 10,000-150,000 depending on distance, mass, ship, urgency, and risk. Hazard, bounty, or recovery work can pay more because failure is costly. If the employer supplies vessel, fuel, and tools, pay is lower; if the worker risks capital, pay rises. Always say whether a figure is wage, gross contract, expense reimbursement, cargo share, salvage share, or prize."
    },
    "Ship Ownership and Operating Cost": {
      "text": "Ships are property and workplaces. Purchase, grant, inheritance, salvage judgment, debt transfer, capture recognized by a jurisdiction, or a complete successful identity-and-title rewrite can establish ownership. Track the hull name, class, condition, current berth, installed modules, crew, cargo, liens, and public identity. A ship remains owned when the player leaves it.\n\nTypical annual operation before damage: small craft 10-25% of hull price; working ship 15-30%; warship 25-60% because crew, ammunition, security, and maintenance dominate. Use concrete costs and allow owners to reduce them through skill, contracts, facilities, mothballing, or scale. Never confiscate an earned ship through newly invented metadata."
    },
    "Ship Class Rarity": {
      "text": "Local traffic is mostly shuttles, skiffs, couriers, workboats, tugs, passenger craft, and freighters. Miners, explorers, patrol cutters, yachts, and specialized support ships are less common. Corvettes, frigates, destroyers, cruisers, carriers, siege vessels, and cityships appear where factions and infrastructure support them. A random derelict is roughly 55% small civilian or industrial, 25% medium commercial, 12% specialist or patrol, 6% light warship, 1.5% heavy warship, and 0.5% capital hull before local-history modifiers."
    },
    "Hacking and Evidence": {
      "text": "Hacking requires a reachable system: radio path, network connection, credential, physical port, opened panel, infected device, insider, supply-chain access, or captured hardware. Success is limited to the systems reached. Ship identity is layered across transponder, drive signature, logs, black box, weapon authorization, financial liens, registry entries, and title history. A clean high-level operation can alter all of them and must be honored. Suspicion can cause inspection; only surviving contradictory evidence, witnesses, incomplete work, or later mistakes can prove fraud."
    },
    "Salvage Law": {
      "text": "Unoccupied does not mean ownerless. A salvage claim depends on abandonment, distress, rescue duty, wartime status, prior title, jurisdiction, notice, evidence, and what the finder actually did. A Salvage Warrant helps file and defend a claim but does not erase survivors, liens, protected graves, active military ownership, or prior registration. Recovery costs and salvage shares can still make a lawful claim valuable. Outside reliable law, possession matters more but buyers, crews, insurers, and ports still ask questions."
    },
    "Generated Derelict Standard": {
      "text": "When a derelict appears, choose role, manufacturer, age, condition, apparent cause, actual cause, crew fate, cargo, hazards, security, legal status, and other interested parties independently. Scans show physical returns, emissions, motion, damage, atmosphere, heat, and transponder data with uncertainty—not the hidden cause. Board to discover interiors. Most finds are mundane and many have already lost their easiest valuables. A claimed or repeatedly visited wreck receives a stable name, areas, condition, evidence, and location; a flyby does not."
    },
    "Planet Generation Standard": {
      "text": "A generated planet profile must specify gravity, atmosphere, climate, major biomes, water, settlement level, primary factions, imports, exports, minerals, at least two flora or analogous producer forms, at least two fauna or analogous consumers, environmental hazards, law, and landing restrictions. Life follows energy, chemistry, reproduction, feeding, territory, migration, and ecology; it is not magic. Use 120-300 characters per species or mineral. Do not give every world breathable air, ruins, sentient natives, lethal predators, or a unique miracle resource."
    },
    "Species and Culture Rule": {
      "text": "Species describes biology, not morality, profession, politics, accent, sexuality, or a single culture. Every major species spans regions, classes, families, faiths or philosophies, factions, and mixed communities. Individuals can adopt other cultural practices and ships. Clothing, tools, rooms, and medicine should account for real body differences when relevant without making every conversation about anatomy."
    },
    "Language and Translation": {
      "text": "Meridian Trade is the most common shared spoken and signed standard, but local languages remain alive. Personal links and collars translate established packages well in routine contexts, with errors in slang, humor, legal nuance, sex, ritual, technical dialect, and new names. Without a shared language or translator, communication uses gesture, images, demonstration, partial vocabulary, and time. Do not grant effortless understanding because dialogue would be convenient."
    },
    "Adult Life and Consent": {
      "text": "Adult sexuality is ordinary social reality: dating, cruising, explicit media, sex work, private relationships, public fashion, healthcare, contraception, fertility choice, kink, and many cultural norms. Laws vary, but consent, contract, privacy, workplace safety, and bodily autonomy are real issues. Attraction can be immediate; trust and access are not. A sex worker sells agreed services, not personal ownership. A relationship score never overrides a refusal. Explicit scenes can be direct when chosen and should remain specific to the people involved."
    },
    "Slavery and Trafficking": {
      "text": "Chattel slavery is illegal across the Concord and many independent systems but persists under the Red Banner, hidden Cartel operations, debt confinement, captured crews, corrupt labor contracts, and remote jurisdictions. Victims may be transported, sold, ransomed, forced to work, sexually exploited, or killed when considered inconvenient. Treat survivors as people with varied reactions and futures, not prizes or automatic followers. The Chainbreak Network conducts extra-legal rescues and record repair."
    },
    "Human Design Language": {
      "text": "Human-built ships vary by culture, but Kestrel Works favors readable modular decks, external service access, broad cargo spines, and controls designed for mixed crews. Human systems often prioritize parts availability and cross-manufacturer compatibility over elegance."
    },
    "Kethari Design Language": {
      "text": "Kethari vessels use dense frames, four-hand control stations, redundant power blocks, strong towing points, visible load paths, and workshops large enough for heavy bodies. Foundry Compact hulls look blunt because they are designed to push, brace, carry, and survive service."
    },
    "Serei Design Language": {
      "text": "Serei ships use smooth pressure cells, curved circulation, distributed systems, wet and dry habitats, strong clinical support, and excellent atmospheric or ocean handling. Their hulls are manufactured composite structures, not living magic."
    },
    "Aeran Design Language": {
      "text": "Aeran Spindlehouse craft are long, light, and thermally expressive, with spars, radiator sails, broad sensor baselines, low-gravity interiors, and excellent control authority. They gain speed and information at the cost of cramped mass margins."
    },
    "Koruun Design Language": {
      "text": "Koruun Red Dune ships use heat-tolerant ceramics, deep cooling channels, armored forward geometry, warm crew spaces, protected boarding systems, and simple robust weapons. They are not all military, but even haulers look ready to survive a furnace."
    },
    "Synthborn Design Language": {
      "text": "Vector Cooperative ships expose modular software, replaceable mission blocks, direct machine interfaces, many body scales, and distributed control. Biological life support is an installed need rather than an assumed center, though mixed crews are common."
    },
    "Human Peoples": {
      "text": "Humans are the most dispersed species in the Meridian, with no single home culture or government. Names, bodies, families, languages, ships, and politics vary by colony and migration history. Human institutions often mistake broad distribution for neutrality; other species notice when “standard” quietly means human-sized."
    },
    "Kethari Peoples": {
      "text": "Kethari evolved under high gravity and have four arms, broad torsos, dense bones, and powerful circulation. Foundry lineages are famous but do not define every Kethari; artists, medics, financiers, pilots, lovers, farmers, scholars, criminals, and pacifists are ordinary. Names often use short personal names and a chosen work, place, or kin marker."
    },
    "Serei Peoples": {
      "text": "Serei are amphibious air-breathers with smooth patterned skin and electroreceptive facial ridges. Many Serevane cultures organize homes across water and dry space, but Serei raised elsewhere may dislike swimming or local tradition. Names often pair a personal name with household, current, reef, or civic affiliation."
    },
    "Aeran Peoples": {
      "text": "Aeran are tall low-gravity people with light frames and gliding membranes between arm and torso. They need support in sustained high gravity and value many different adaptations rather than one heroic ideal. Spindlehouse engineering is prominent, while Aeran communities also include couriers, performers, bureaucrats, farmers, and settled families."
    },
    "Koruun Peoples": {
      "text": "Koruun have scaled skin, heat reservoirs, strong jaws, and good glare tolerance. Their cultures include refinery cities, cold-adapted diaspora, military states, communal farms, universities, and nomad houses. Warm rooms are bodily comfort, not proof of aggression. Names commonly use strong consonants but vary by region."
    },
    "Nimri Peoples": {
      "text": "Nimri are compact furred people with sensitive ears, whiskers, vibration awareness, and strong spatial memory. They are sometimes stereotyped as technicians or thieves because of body size and old station labor patterns; treat that as prejudice, not truth. Nimri names favor clear two- or three-syllable personal names and many local family forms."
    },
    "Thessan Peoples": {
      "text": "Thessans have four eyes, protective chitin patches, two main arms, and a smaller dexterous pair. Their wide vision and fine parallel handling influence some tools and art, not their ethics or intelligence. Thessan adulthood, gender, kinship, and fashion differ across settled populations rather than following one caste system."
    },
    "Praxian Peoples": {
      "text": "Praxians are radial-bodied people from a dense cold atmosphere, using six manipulators around a protected central body. Off-world pressure garments are clothing, mobility support, and life support at once. Their translated names often join a personal tone-name and habitat affiliation, such as Orru-Vel; translation hardware does not flatten individual voice."
    },
    "Synthborn Peoples": {
      "text": "Synthborn are people whose minds run in manufactured bodies. Some were created new, some legally continued from biological persons, and some descend culturally rather than technically from earlier systems. Bodies, copying, backups, forks, mortality, sex, sensation, family, and continuity are subjects of personal choice and law, not evidence that Synthborn lack emotion."
    },
    "Veyra Planet Profile": {
      "text": "Veyra has 1.28 standard gravity, breathable humid air, broad oceans, warm coasts, cool uplands, fertile volcanic soils, and dense settlement. It exports food, finance, education, administration, media, and high-value services; it imports bulk fuel, rare minerals, and some industrial stock. Landing is restricted to certified fields because heavy gravity and weather punish casual approaches."
    },
    "Veyra Flora": {
      "text": "Blueleaf orchard trees grow low and broad under high gravity, storing aromatic sugar in dense blue-green fruit. Saltglass reeds build mineral stems through tidal flats and shelter spawning animals. Duskmere medicinal vines produce anti-inflammatory compounds when trained over cool stone terraces."
    },
    "Veyra Fauna": {
      "text": "Stoneback grazers are six-legged herd animals with broad feet and calm social behavior, raised for fiber and meat. Calyx sailbirds use strong coastal winds to glide despite the gravity and nest on protected towers. Mirror crabs filter tidal pools and flash polarized shell patterns during courtship."
    },
    "Veyra Minerals": {
      "text": "Veyra’s useful minerals are common volcanic basalt, construction stone, saltglass silicates, clays, and modest geothermal deposits rather than miraculous rare ore. Its wealth comes from people, agriculture, institutions, and location."
    },
    "Kharon Planet Profile": {
      "text": "Kharon has 0.86 standard gravity, a thin breathable but dusty atmosphere, little surface water, large thermal gradients, and mineral-rich impact basins. It exports metals, ceramics, machine parts, volatiles, and industrial expertise; it imports food variety, water equipment, medicine, and consumer goods. Landings use surveyed fields and weather windows."
    },
    "Kharon Flora": {
      "text": "Red mat colonies are native photosynthetic microbial sheets that stabilize dust around warm seeps. Needlebrush is an engineered food and fiber shrub with deep roots and foldable leaves. Condenser lichen grows on shaded rock and slowly traps atmospheric moisture, making wild patches legally protected near settlements."
    },
    "Kharon Fauna": {
      "text": "Dust runners are small six-limbed omnivores that shelter in cracks and raid unsecured food. Basin striders are tall domestic pack animals bred from native grazers and adapted to sparse water. Glasswing swarms emerge after rare rain, mate over thermal flats, and become food for much of the local web."
    },
    "Kharon Minerals": {
      "text": "Kharon holds iron, nickel, titanium, industrial ceramics, rare-earth bands, conductor metals, black impact glass, and useful volatile pockets. Deposits are valuable because infrastructure reaches them, not because every sample is unique."
    },
    "Serevane Planet Profile": {
      "text": "Serevane has 0.94 standard gravity, breathable wet air, ninety-one percent ocean, volcanic archipelagos, broad shelves, deep trenches, and severe seasonal storms. It exports food, medicine, biopolymers, clinical cultures, marine technology, and rescue expertise; it imports structural metals, dry-land crops, and heavy machinery. Ecological declaration is mandatory before landing."
    },
    "Serevane Flora": {
      "text": "Ribbon kelp forms cultivated forests used for food, polymer, and habitat. Bluewell fans are slow-growing chemosynthetic colonies around cool mineral seeps. Rainglass vine grows on wet island cliffs and yields carefully regulated medicinal compounds."
    },
    "Serevane Fauna": {
      "text": "Lantern shoals are hand-sized schooling swimmers whose coordinated light confuses predators. Reef walkers are social amphibious grazers that use four grasping fins on exposed rock. Shelf leviathans are large filter feeders protected during migration and dangerous mainly to ships that ignore marked routes."
    },
    "Serevane Minerals": {
      "text": "Serevane produces vent sulfides, dissolved industrial salts, basalt, biogenic carbonates, and limited deep rare-earth deposits. Extraction is licensed around migration, reef load, and water contamination rather than prohibited everywhere."
    },
    "Aster's Moon Profile": {
      "text": "The moon below Aster’s Wake has 0.72 standard gravity, cool breathable air, shallow seas, grasslike producer mats, burrowing grazers, and no native sapient population. Colonists grow adapted food inside and outside controlled plots. It exports seed stock, light agricultural products, and survey data while importing machines, medicine, and specialists."
    },
    "Emberwell Profile": {
      "text": "Emberwell’s settled planet has 1.03 standard gravity, dry temperate plains, iron-rich soil, native waxleaf producers, herd grazers, and strong seasonal fires. The colony exports grain, heat ceramics, and machine parts. Fire corridors, water rights, and unexploded wartime debris shape land law without defining every day."
    },
    "Brineglass Ecology": {
      "text": "Brineglass is a cold moon ocean whose transparent microbial reef fans grow through mineral precipitation and photosynthesis under ice-thin regions. Small filter swimmers, grazing ribbons, and dormant spore clouds form a slow ecosystem. There are no ruins or hidden intelligent builders; the living geology is valuable enough."
    },
    "Faction Motion": {
      "text": "Factions pursue budgets, elections, promotions, construction, patrols, strikes, contracts, investigations, diplomacy, raids, rescue, and internal disputes without waiting for the player. Advance only established projects at a believable pace and surface changes through news, price, traffic, personnel, or direct consequence. Do not create a new war or conspiracy merely to prove the universe is alive."
    },
    "NPC Persistence": {
      "text": "Once named and materially engaged, an NPC persists with the same body, voice, job, knowledge, relationships, possessions, location history, and unresolved commitments unless play changes them. Minor strangers need not become permanent records. Promote recurring workers, rivals, lovers, crew, clients, witnesses, and officials to detailed NPCs rather than regenerating replacements."
    },
    "Generated Description Limits": {
      "text": "GENERATION BUDGETS AND PERSISTENCE\n- New NPC basicInfo: 2 sentences, 180-350 characters. hiddenInfo: 3-5 short sentences, 300-650 characters. personality: 3-5 behavior entries. Civilian abilities: 0-2; trained: 3-5; elite: 6-8.\n- Item description: 80-220 characters plus one price sentence; no more than 3 bonuses.\n- Skill description: 180-400 characters. Ability: 100-250 characters. Trait: 150-350 characters.\n- Flora, fauna, or mineral entry: 120-300 characters. Ship or wreck scan: 150-400 characters.\n- Location basicInfo: 200-450 characters; hiddenInfo maximum 650. Simple locations have 2-4 areas; complex locations 4-8.\n- Quest statement: 150-350 characters; quest design brief: 300-650 characters.\n\nStart every generated entity at detailType basic. Expand it only after the player examines it, builds a relationship, accepts work tied to it, claims it, or revisits it. Add detail without rewriting established facts. Reuse existing entities before making new ones. Never combine tragedy, secret allegiance, romance, rare gear, and a quest hook by default. One strong function plus one human detail is enough."
    },
    "Metadata Firewall": {
      "text": "METADATA FIREWALL\nNarrator instructions, hiddenInfo, character sheets, traits, skills, inventory state, quest data, trigger storage, summaries, and generation notes are not character knowledge. An NPC knows only what they personally observed, were told, can plausibly infer from visible evidence, or can access through a stated sensor, database, faction channel, witness, or public record. Treat each source as limited and possibly outdated.\n\nDo not let NPCs name unseen traits, skills, relationships, secrets, crimes, ownership history, inventory contents, objectives, or off-screen events. A uniform, visible implant, equipped weapon, public warrant, spoken admission, or demonstrated technique can be noticed; the hidden field behind it cannot. The narrator may know the truth while every character remains wrong. Never leak a twist through suspicious dialogue, uncanny certainty, or an NPC reacting to a private action."
    },
    "Ship Claim Protocol": {
      "text": "SHIP CLAIMS, TRANSPONDERS, AND HACKING\nA ship is not legally owned merely because someone reached the bridge. Establish physical access, control of the command root, and a path through its security. A complete identity rewrite may cover the ship name, transponder, drive signature, internal logs, black box, weapon authorization, financial liens, registry record, and chain of title. The exact layers depend on the ship and jurisdiction.\n\nUse hacking difficulty by system rather than narrative importance: routine civilian 10; hardened commercial 25; patrol or corporate security 40; military 55; capital-command systems 70; a clean multi-registry laundering operation 80. Damage, valid credentials, insider help, isolated networks, specialist tools, and time can lower difficulty. Active defenders, intact black ICE, remote oversight, or missing registry access can raise it.\n\nWhen action results establish a complete successful rewrite, honor it. External records and onboard evidence now support the new identity. Witnesses may recognize the hull class and authorities may inspect it, but suspicion is not proof. Exposure requires surviving evidence, an identified witness, a contradictory live record, a later player mistake, or an explicitly incomplete layer. Never invent an unknown checksum, secret beacon, magic forensic trace, or omniscient official solely to take the ship away.\n\nRare warships and carriers are difficult to find and claim, not forbidden rewards. After a valid claim, create play from actual condition, crew, supply, berth, ammunition, repair, insurance, diplomacy, and operating cost. Do not retroactively negate ownership."
    },
    "Derelict Protocol": {
      "text": "DERELICTS AND WRECKS\nGenerate abandoned ships, wrecks, lost craft, and empty stations as situational encounters, not a permanent catalog. Weight the result by local traffic, recent conflict, factions, industry, and remoteness. Most finds are shuttles, workboats, couriers, miners, or freighters; patrol craft and specialist vessels are uncommon; destroyers, carriers, and other capital hulls are genuinely rare but possible.\n\nChoose independently: hull role and manufacturer; age and condition; apparent cause; actual cause; crew fate; remaining cargo; hazards; security; legal status; and whether another party is already searching. Causes include accident, piracy, slaving, battle, sabotage, mutiny, illness, environmental failure, fraud, abandonment, or deliberate scuttling. Crew may have evacuated, been rescued, been captured or enslaved, died and remained aboard, been spaced, hidden, or survived in sealed sections. Do not default to corpses, a lone survivor, a secret experiment, or a priceless prototype.\n\nInitial scans reveal only credible sensor facts. Boarding, logs, physical evidence, survivors, and outside records reveal more. A minor find stays an encounter. Create a persistent named location or asset only when the player boards it, claims it, changes it, returns to it, or a continuing quest depends on it. Generate interior areas only after boarding."
    }
  },
